From e860ae4b172f888d0e1c52f7c3143f0d95ccd61a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 12:59:32 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Wayland: follow a window's depth for the elements that
 declare their own

A widget may declare rectangles inside it at their own depth (Qt Widgets,
_q_stereo_depth_rects). Such a window has to know, from the compositor,
whether the elements inside it may be shown in depth now and how far one
step of depth inside a window goes, whatever style draws it.

Qt Widgets sets the window's _q_stereo_follow_depth through the native
interface. The window then makes a kde_stereo_depth_v1 object of its own
for its surface, on every surface it gets, and the compositor's word
reaches the window as two properties: _q_stereo_awake (depth_state) and
_q_stereo_element_limit, the person's element limit in pixels of a view
1920 wide (element_limit, version 7 of kde_stereo_content_v1). With a
compositor older than version 7 the window never hears an element limit,
its elements are drawn flat, and the log says why, once per window.

The protocol copy is version 7 (plasma-wayland-protocols +stereo3d13,
the same file), and the manager is bound at the compositor's version up to
7. A window that declares nothing makes no new object, so every other
window talks to the compositor as before.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
